WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.490 --> 00:00:04.130
The same query costs ten times more on a bad table.

2
00:00:04.150 --> 00:00:08.515
Storage choices decide the cost and the speed of everything above them.

3
00:00:08.535 --> 00:00:13.215
Three layers to know: the file, the table format, and the layout.

4
00:00:13.235 --> 00:00:14.140
First: the file.

5
00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:15.400
Parquet.

6
00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:18.610
Parquet stores data by column, not by row.

7
00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:21.310
A query that needs three columns reads only three.

8
00:00:21.330 --> 00:00:23.445
The rest stays on disk.

9
00:00:23.465 --> 00:00:26.282
Each block also keeps a minimum and a maximum.

10
00:00:26.302 --> 00:00:29.145
So the reader can skip whole blocks.

11
00:00:29.165 --> 00:00:31.805
Second: files alone are not a table.

12
00:00:31.825 --> 00:00:33.468
Two jobs write at once.

13
00:00:33.488 --> 00:00:35.005
A reader sees half of each.

14
00:00:35.025 --> 00:00:36.890
That is a broken table.

15
00:00:36.910 --> 00:00:41.325
A table format keeps a list of the files that make the table, right now.

16
00:00:41.345 --> 00:00:43.550
A commit swaps that list in one step.

17
00:00:43.570 --> 00:00:47.750
So you get transactions, snapshots, and time travel.

18
00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:50.610
Third: lay out the table for its filters.

19
00:00:50.630 --> 00:00:53.347
Partition by the column people filter on most.

20
00:00:53.367 --> 00:00:55.445
Often, that is the date.

21
00:00:55.465 --> 00:00:57.945
But too many partitions give you tiny files.

22
00:00:57.965 --> 00:01:00.030
And tiny files are slow.

23
00:01:00.050 --> 00:01:04.230
So compact small files into bigger ones, on a schedule.

24
00:01:04.250 --> 00:01:06.840
Last: what do you pay for in BigQuery?

25
00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:10.775
With on-demand pricing, you pay for the bytes a query scans.

26
00:01:10.795 --> 00:01:12.900
So partition and cluster the table.

27
00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:15.725
And never select every column.

28
00:01:15.745 --> 00:01:18.385
Quick recap of storage and the lakehouse.

29
00:01:18.405 --> 00:01:20.147
Parquet reads by column.

30
00:01:20.167 --> 00:01:22.335
A table format adds transactions.

31
00:01:22.355 --> 00:01:24.620
And compact your small files.

32
00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:27.740
Now start the first lesson below.
